        "content":  "MUN basics\r\nMUN BASICS – QUICK NOTES (BEGINNER FRIENDLY)\r\n\r\n1. Mods vs Unmods\r\n\r\nModerated Caucus (Mods)\r\n\r\nFormal but flexible discussion.\r\nThe person proposing or chair sets:\r\nTopic\r\nSpeaking time (e.g., 30–60 seconds)\r\nDelegates speak one-by-one.\r\nUsed to:\r\nDebate specific subtopics\r\nPush arguments\r\nTest ideas publicly\r\nUnmoderated Caucus (Unmods)\r\n\r\nInformal discussion.\r\nDelegates move around and talk freely.\r\nNo speaking order or anything, proper informal chats.\r\nUsed to:\r\nNegotiate\r\nForm blocs\r\nWrite resolutions/draft clauses\r\nKey distinction:\r\n\r\nMods = public speaking\r\nUnmods = deal-making + writing. More politicking.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n2. Types of Points (Know these cold)\r\n\r\nPoint of Personal Privilege\r\n\r\nExample: “Could the delegate please speak louder?”\r\nOr going to the resteroom if you are meant to ask perms.\r\nPoint of Order\r\n\r\nUse when rules are not being followed.\r\nExample: “Chair, speaking time exceeded.”\r\nMust relate to procedure, not content.\r\nPoint of Parliamentary Inquiry\r\n\r\nAsk the chair about the procedure.\r\nExample: “How long is the voting procedure?”\r\nRight of Reply\r\n\r\nRequest if your country is directly insulted.\r\nGranted at the chair’s discretion.\r\n\r\n3. Motions (Core ones)\r\n\r\nYou’ll constantly raise these:\r\n\r\nMotion for Moderated Caucus\r\nMotion for Unmoderated Caucus\r\nMotion to Open/Close Debate\r\nMotion to Enter Voting Procedure\r\nStructure of a motion:\r\n“Motion for a moderated caucus of X minutes, speaking time Y seconds, on the topic of Z.”\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nHow to Argue Diplomatically.\r\nThese are how you structure content:\r\n\r\nAssertion\r\n\r\nClaim you are making.\r\nReasoning\r\n\r\nWhy is your claim true?\r\nEvidence\r\n\r\nFacts, examples, stats.\r\nImpact\r\n\r\nWhy it matters.\r\nSimple structure:\r\nClaim → Why → Example → So what do we gotta do about that\r\n\r\nGSL (General Speakers List) – In-Depth\r\nMain speaking queue.\r\nSet at the start of the committee.\r\nContinues throughout unless paused.\r\nUsed for:\r\nOpening positions\r\nBig-picture arguments\r\nSetting narrative\r\n7. Basic Structure for a GSL Speech\r\nKeep it tight and repeatable:\r\n\r\n1. Hook (1–2 lines)\r\n\r\nFrame the issue.\r\nExample: “The crisis we face is not a lack of resources, but a failure of coordination.”\r\n2. Country Position\r\n\r\nWhat your country believes.\r\nClear stance.\r\n3. 2–3 Key Points\r\nEach point:\r\n\r\nClaim\r\nReason\r\nExample\r\nImpact\r\n4. Solution Direction\r\n\r\nBroad (not detailed clauses yet).\r\nSignals leadership.\r\n5. Closing\r\n\r\nStrong, memorable line. It’s usually nice if it links to the claim made in the start clearly.\r\n8. What Actually Wins You Committees\r\nIgnore fluff. Focus on:\r\n\r\nSpeaking often (mods + GSL)\r\nControlling narrative early\r\nLeading blocs in unmods\r\nWriting strong, clear clauses\r\nBeing logically consistent\r\n9. Minimal Working Strategy\r\nGet on GSL immediately.\r\nPush for mods you control.\r\nUse unmods to build a bloc fast.\r\nNote every influencial/prominent delegates core points.\r\nSpeak like you already lead."
