        "content":  "================================================================\nMUN RESEARCH WORKSPACE: README \u0026 PROJECT GUIDE\n================================================================\n\nThis workspace contains a comprehensive research library and debate-ready resources for two distinct committees:\n\n1. UNHRC (United Nations Human Rights Council)\n   Agenda: Human Rights Implications of Artificial Intelligence, Mass Digital Surveillance, and Data Privacy.\n\n2. UNSC (United Nations Security Council)\n   Agenda: Impact of Ongoing Geopolitical Conflicts on Global Supply Chains, Maritime Security, and Free Trade.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nDIRECTORY NAVIGATION\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n\nMUN/\n├── README.txt                       \u003c- This index and usage guide\n│\n├── UNHRC/                           \u003c- Human Rights Council Folder\n│   ├── 00 Agenda Handbook.txt       \u003c- Comprehensive agenda handbook\n│   ├── Countries/                   \u003c- Detailed country positions (15 files)\n│   ├── Speeches/                    \u003c- Debate speech templates and GSL notes\n│   ├── Resolutions/                 \u003c- Working paper and draft clauses\n│   └── Resources/                   \u003c- Legal frameworks, statistics, definitions\n│\n└── UNSC/                            \u003c- Security Council Folder\n    ├── 00 Agenda Handbook.txt       \u003c- Maritime and supply chain handbook\n    ├── Countries/                   \u003c- Detailed country positions (20 files)\n    ├── Speeches/                    \u003c- GSL and moderated caucus templates\n    ├── Resolutions/                 \u003c- Resolution clauses and solutions\n    └── Resources/                   \u003c- Maritime treaties, charter guides, statistics\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nHOW TO USE THESE RESOURCES IN COMMITTEE\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n1. DURING THE GENERAL SPEAKER\u0027S LIST (GSL):\n   - Refer to [Countries/] directories for your country\u0027s GSL outline.\n   - Use the [Speeches/General GSL.txt] templates to quickly adapt your opening remarks to the flow of debate.\n\n2. DURING MODERATED CAUCUSES:\n   - Go to [Speeches/Moderated Caucus.txt] under your committee\u0027s folder for pre-written arguments, speaking notes, and statistics tailored to specific sub-issues (e.g., Red Sea corridors, facial recognition, or data localization).\n\n3. DURING UNMODERATED CAUCUSES:\n   - Use [Resolutions/Working Paper Clauses.txt] and [Solutions.txt] to guide your bloc\u0027s drafting process. Copy and adapt these pre-formatted clauses directly into your working papers.\n\n4. DURING QUESTIONS \u0026 ANSWERS (Q\u0026A):\n   - Check the \"LIKELY QUESTIONS AGAINST THIS COUNTRY\" in your country file to prepare defensive arguments.\n   - Use the \"QUESTIONS THIS COUNTRY CAN ASK\" section to challenge and lobby opposing blocs.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nOFFICIAL SOURCES\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n• United Nations Digital Library: https://digitallibrary.un.org\n• International Maritime Organization (IMO): https://www.imo.org\n• World Trade Organization (WTO): https://www.wto.org\n• UN Conference on Trade and Development (UNCTAD): https://unctad.org\n• Office of the High Commissioner for Human Rights (OHCHR): https://www.ohchr.org\n\n================================================================\n"
