        "content":  "UNITED STATES OF AMERICA\r\n\r\n================================================================\r\nQUICK SUMMARY\r\n================================================================\r\nThe United States is the world\u0027s largest economy and a dominant military power with significant global influence across all international institutions. It plays a central role in the UN Security Council as a P5 member and is a leading voice on technology, human rights, and international security.\r\n\r\n================================================================\r\nBASIC INFORMATION\r\n================================================================\r\nCapital: Washington, D.C.\r\nLargest City: New York City\r\nOfficial Name: United States of America\r\nGovernment Type: Federal presidential constitutional republic\r\nHead of State: President Joe Biden\r\nHead of Government: President Joe Biden\r\nOfficial Languages: None at federal level; English (de facto)\r\nPopulation: ~340 million (2025)\r\nArea: 9,833,520 km² (3rd largest)\r\nCurrency: United States Dollar (USD)\r\nGDP (Nominal): ~$28.8 trillion (2025)\r\nGDP Per Capita: ~$83,000\r\n\r\n================================================================\r\nECONOMY\r\n================================================================\r\nMajor Industries: Technology, financial services, healthcare, defense, aerospace, energy, agriculture, entertainment\r\nMajor Exports: Refined petroleum, crude oil, aircraft, vehicles, integrated circuits, pharmaceuticals\r\nMajor Imports: Vehicles, crude oil, computers, broadcasting equipment, pharmaceuticals\r\nMajor Trade Partners: Canada, Mexico, China, Japan, Germany, United Kingdom, South Korea\r\n\r\n================================================================\r\nMILITARY \u0026 SECURITY\r\n================================================================\r\nMilitary Overview: Largest military budget globally (~$920 billion). Operates the most capable navy, air force, and nuclear arsenal. Maintains ~750 military bases across 80+ countries.\r\nNuclear Weapons Status: Recognized nuclear weapon state (NPT). ~5,550 warheads (deployed + stockpiled).\r\nUN Membership: Founding member (1945)\r\nUNSC Status: P5 (permanent member with veto power)\r\nMajor International Organizations: UN, NATO, G7, G20, OECD, OAS, IMF, World Bank, WTO\r\n\r\n================================================================\r\nGEOGRAPHY \u0026 INFLUENCE\r\n================================================================\r\nRegional Influence: Dominant power in North and South America; extensive influence in Europe, East Asia, and Middle East\r\nNeighbouring Countries: Canada (north), Mexico (south)\r\nStrategic Importance: Controls key global sea lanes through naval power; hosts global financial system (USD reserve currency); leads NATO alliance\r\nImportant Maritime Routes: Atlantic Ocean, Pacific Ocean, Gulf of Mexico, Arctic routes, Panama Canal access\r\nImportant Geography: Two ocean borders provide strategic insulation; vast agricultural heartland; significant oil and gas reserves in Texas, Alaska, Gulf of Mexico\r\n\r\n================================================================\r\nENERGY \u0026 RESOURCES\r\n================================================================\r\nEnergy Resources: Largest producer of oil and natural gas globally; leading producer of renewable energy (wind, solar); extensive nuclear power capacity\r\nNatural Resources: Coal, copper, gold, uranium, timber, rare earth minerals, natural gas, petroleum\r\nTechnology Sector: World leader — Silicon Valley, major AI and semiconductor companies (Apple, Microsoft, Google, Amazon, Nvidia, Intel)\r\nMajor Companies: Apple, Microsoft, Alphabet (Google), Amazon, Nvidia, Meta, Tesla, Berkshire Hathaway, JPMorgan Chase, ExxonMobil\r\n\r\n================================================================\r\nSOCIETY\r\n================================================================\r\nEducation Level: High — 90%+ adult literacy; ~50% hold tertiary degrees\r\nInternet Penetration: ~93% of population\r\nHuman Development Index (HDI): 0.921 (Very High, ranked 20th)\r\nCorruption Perceptions Index: 69/100 (ranked 24th)\r\nPress Freedom Status: Mostly free; ranked ~45th globally by RSF — concerns over media polarization\r\nHuman Rights Overview: Strong constitutional protections but faces challenges in racial equality, mass incarceration, gun violence, and surveillance law reforms\r\n\r\n================================================================\r\nFOREIGN POLICY\r\n================================================================\r\nCurrent Leader Priorities: Democracy reinforcement abroad, climate action, strategic competition with China, managing Russia-Ukraine war, technology regulation, immigration reform\r\nForeign Policy Overview: Multilateralism (Biden admin); emphasis on alliances (NATO, Quad, AUKUS); strategic competition with China; support for Ukraine; engagement in Middle East peace processes\r\nRelations With:\r\n  USA: —\r\n  China: Strategic competitor; intense rivalry in trade, technology, Taiwan, and South China Sea\r\n  Russia: Adversarial — sanctions regime, support for Ukraine, arms control tensions\r\n  India: Strategic partner; growing defence and technology cooperation (Quad, iCET)\r\n  Pakistan: Fluctuating — security cooperation on Afghanistan, but tensions over terrorism and nuclear issues\r\n  EU: Strong alliance; NATO and trade partnership; coordination on China and Russia\r\n  Middle East: Key ally of Israel; engages with Saudi Arabia, UAE, and Gulf states; nuclear deal tensions with Iran\r\n  Neighbouring Countries: Strong economic integration with Canada and Mexico (USMCA)\r\n\r\n================================================================\r\nALLIANCES \u0026 RIVALRIES\r\n================================================================\r\nMajor Allies: NATO members, Japan, South Korea, Australia, Israel, Saudi Arabia, India (strategic partner)\r\nMajor Rivals: China, Russia, Iran, North Korea\r\n\r\n================================================================\r\nNATIONAL INTERESTS \u0026 CHALLENGES\r\n================================================================\r\nKey National Interests: Maintain global economic and military leadership; protect critical infrastructure; secure supply chains; advance democracy and human rights; counter terrorism; address climate change\r\nCurrent Major Challenges: Political polarization; national debt ($34+ trillion); China\u0027s rising influence; immigration policy; healthcare costs; gun violence; technology regulation (AI, social media)\r\n\r\n================================================================\r\nINTERESTING FACTS\r\n================================================================\r\n• The US has not had a federal official language.\r\n• The US dollar is the world\u0027s primary reserve currency (~58% of global reserves).\r\n• The US spends more on its military than the next 10 countries combined.\r\n• Silicon Valley produces most of the world\u0027s leading AI and technology companies.\r\n\r\n================================================================\r\nSOURCES\r\n================================================================\r\n• CIA World Factbook — United States\r\n• World Bank — US Country Data\r\n• IMF — World Economic Outlook Database\r\n• SIPRI — Military Expenditure Database\r\n• UNDP — Human Development Report\r\n• Transparency International — Corruption Perceptions Index\r\n• US Department of State\r\n"
