        "content":  "================================================================\r\nUNSC SOLUTIONS PORTFOLIO: MARITIME SECURITY \u0026 TRADE | BATTLE CARD\r\n================================================================\r\n\r\n⚡ QUICK REFERENCE\r\n   Committee: UNSC — Agenda: Geopolitical Conflicts, Supply Chains \u0026 Maritime Security\r\n   Strategy: Propose 1-2 solutions → Gauge P5 veto risk → Incorporate into operative clauses\r\n   Easiest Consensus: Solution 3 (Undersea Infrastructure) — broadest cross-bloc support\r\n\r\nThis document details four debate-ready solutions for resolutions addressing maritime safety and global supply chains.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\nSOLUTION 1: UN MARITIME SECURITY COORDINATION MECHANISM\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• Description: Establishing a centralized, UN-coordinated system to bridge communications between different naval coalitions (e.g., Operation Prosperity Guardian, Operation Aspides, and non-Western patrols) and commercial shipping.\r\n• Key Elements:\r\n  - Real-time transit tracking and threat alerts.\r\n  - Joint protocols for responding to distress signals in conflict zones.\r\n  - De-confliction zones near major choke points.\r\n• Bloc Support: Supported by transit states and Western allies.\r\n• Opposed by: States concerned with foreign intelligence access to shipping databases.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\nSOLUTION 2: SHADOW FLEET SAFETY MORATORIUM\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• Description: Requiring all vessels carrying hazardous cargo (such as crude oil) through international straits to verify P\u0026I insurance and compliance with IMO structural safety rules (double-hull requirements).\r\n• Key Elements:\r\n  - Coastal state inspections of non-compliant vessels at strait entrances.\r\n  - Centralized UN registry of verified, insured tankers.\r\n  - Establishing environmental response teams in high-risk zones.\r\n• Bloc Support: Sanctions-enforcing Western states.\r\n• Opposed by: Russia and states operating shadow fleet logistics.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\nSOLUTION 3: UNDERSEA INFRASTRUCTURE PROTECTION PROTOCOLS\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• Description: Creating a joint monitoring framework to protect undersea fiber-optic communication cables and gas pipelines in international waters.\r\n• Key Elements:\r\n  - Sound sensor arrays and satellite tracking of suspicious vessels lingering over cables.\r\n  - Rapid-repair agreements to deploy technicians during sabotage events.\r\n  - Sovereign control over maintenance remains within EEZs.\r\n• Bloc Support: Broad consensus, though states reject sharing sensitive naval operations data.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\nSOLUTION 4: TRADE CORRIDOR DIVERSIFICATION AND CAPACITY BUILDING\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• Description: Supporting the development of alternative transport corridors (such as the Middle Corridor or North-South Corridor) to de-risk global trade from single choke point bottlenecks.\r\n• Key Elements:\r\n  - Financial support from the World Bank to modernize rail networks in Central Asia.\r\n  - Simplification of border customs protocols through digital registries.\r\n  - Strengthening port efficiency in developing transit states.\r\n• Bloc Support: Supported by Global South, landlocked Central Asian states, and China.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\nOFFICIAL SOURCES\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• International Maritime Organization (IMO) Conventions\r\n• UNCTAD Maritime Transport Studies\r\n================================================================\r\n"
