        "content":  "================================================================\r\nUNSC WORKING PAPER CLAUSES: MARITIME SECURITY \u0026 TRADE | BATTLE CARD\r\n================================================================\r\n\r\n⚡ QUICK REFERENCE\r\n   Committee: UNSC — Agenda: Geopolitical Conflicts, Supply Chains \u0026 Maritime Security\r\n   Strategy: Start with 5-6 preambular clauses → Add 5 operative → Merge with allies\r\n   Tip: Use these as your opening merge document to control the drafting process\r\n\r\nThis document provides sample preambular and operative clauses for use during the initial drafting phase of working papers in the Security Council.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\nSAMPLE PREAMBULAR CLAUSES (Context and framing)\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n\r\n• Reaffirming that freedom of navigation, as established under the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS), is essential for global trade and international security,\r\n\r\n• Deeply concerned by the escalating security threats in the Gulf of Aden, Bab-el-Mandeb, and Red Sea transit corridors, which disrupt global supply chains and force ships to route around the Cape of Good Hope,\r\n\r\n• Acknowledging the severe economic impact of shipping disruptions on transit revenue-dependent states, specifically noting the loss of revenue for the Suez Canal Authority,\r\n\r\n• Emphasizing the vulnerability of critical maritime infrastructure, including undersea communication cables and offshore energy terminals, to sabotage and cyber interception,\r\n\r\n• Recognizing that stable global food and fertilizer supply chains are critical to preventing humanitarian crises in import-dependent nations,\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\nSAMPLE OPERATIVE CLAUSES (Action mandates)\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n\r\n1. Encourages member states to coordinate information sharing through regional maritime intelligence hubs to detect and prevent piracy, smuggling, and threats to merchant transit;\r\n\r\n2. Recommends that coastal and transit states implement the IMO\u0027s International Ship and Port Facility Security (ISPS) Code to strengthen port cybersecurity defenses;\r\n\r\n3. Calls upon states to participate in regional maritime search-and-rescue (SAR) exercises to build trust and improve disaster response capabilities along busy sea lanes;\r\n\r\n4. Invites developing nations to seek financial and technical assistance from international development banks to modernize port infrastructure and cargo screening systems;\r\n\r\n5. Encourages the expansion of grain transit corridors in the Black Sea and Danube regions through coordinated de-mining initiatives.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\nOFFICIAL SOURCES\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• United Nations Security Council Editorial Manual\r\n• International Maritime Organization Assembly Guides\r\n================================================================\r\n"
