        "content":  "================================================================\r\nUNSC RESOURCE GUIDE: GLOBAL CASE STUDIES | BATTLE CARD\r\n================================================================\r\n\r\n⚡ QUICK REFERENCE\r\n   Committee: UNSC — Agenda: Geopolitical Conflicts, Supply Chains \u0026 Maritime Security\r\n   Strategy: Red Sea Houthi (demand action) + Black Sea Grain (prove mediation works)\r\n           Nord Stream (undersea vulnerability) + Ever Given (choke point risk)\r\n           Combine Red Sea + Ever Given = strongest case for corridor diversification\r\n\r\nReference these case studies to support your arguments regarding maritime security and supply chain vulnerabilities.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\nCASE STUDY 1: THE RED SEA HOUTHI ATTACKS (2023-PRESENT)\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• Description: Following geopolitical conflict in Gaza, Yemeni Houthi forces launched drone and missile attacks on commercial vessels transiting the Bab-el-Mandeb Strait, declaring a blockade on Israel-bound shipping.\r\n• Impact:\r\n  - Forced major shipping firms (Maersk, MSC) to divert ships around Africa.\r\n  - Caused a 40% reduction in Suez Canal transit revenues, severely affecting Egypt\u0027s economy.\r\n  - Led to the establishment of US-led Operation Prosperity Guardian and EU Operation Aspides.\r\n• Debate Use: Cite to argue for international task forces to protect trade lanes and coordinate navigation security.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\nCASE STUDY 2: NORD STREAM PIPELINE SABOTAGE (2022)\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• Description: Underwater explosions ruptured three of the four Nord Stream natural gas pipelines in the Baltic Sea within Denmark and Sweden\u0027s EEZ, releasing massive amounts of methane.\r\n• Impact:\r\n  - Destroyed direct gas delivery routes from Russia to Germany, forcing a rapid energy supply chain shift to LNG imports.\r\n  - Highlighted the extreme vulnerability of undersea critical energy and data infrastructure.\r\n• Debate Use: Cite to demand coordinated UN-led protection for undersea cables and pipelines in international waters.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\nCASE STUDY 3: THE BLACK SEA GRAIN INITIATIVE (2022-2023)\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• Description: A UN and Turkey-brokered agreement between Russia and Ukraine to permit the safe transit of agricultural exports from three Ukrainian Black Sea ports.\r\n• Impact:\r\n  - Allowed over 32 million metric tons of grain to reach global markets, reducing global food prices by 20%.\r\n  - Terminated by Russia in July 2023, who cited obstacles to its own agricultural and fertilizer exports.\r\n• Debate Use: Cite to prove that neutral mediation and UN-brokered trade corridors are effective alternatives to military escorts.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\nCASE STUDY 4: THE SUEZ CANAL BLOCKAGE (2021)\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• Description: The ultra-large container ship Ever Given ran aground and blocked the Suez Canal for six days, halting over 400 ships.\r\n• Impact:\r\n  - Blocked an estimated $9.6 billion worth of trade daily.\r\n  - Demonstrated the high risk of single choke point dependencies.\r\n• Debate Use: Cite when arguing for trade corridor diversification and investment in alternate routes (such as Central Asian rail links).\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\nOFFICIAL SOURCES\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• UNCTAD Trade and Development Reports\r\n• International Maritime Organization Safety Incident Circulars\r\n• World Bank Food Security Updates\r\n================================================================\r\n"
