        "path":  "UNSC/Resources/Maritime Law.txt",
        "name":  "Maritime Law.txt",
        "size":  3478,
        "content":  "================================================================\r\nUNSC RESOURCE GUIDE: KEY MARITIME LAW CONVENTIONS | BATTLE CARD\r\n================================================================\r\n\r\n⚡ QUICK REFERENCE\r\n   Committee: UNSC — Agenda: Geopolitical Conflicts, Supply Chains \u0026 Maritime Security\r\n   Key Cites: Montreux Convention (Black Sea) / SUA Convention (maritime terrorism)\r\n           SOLAS (AIS transponders - shadow fleet violations) / MARPOL (pollution - aging tankers)\r\n\r\nThis document outlines key conventions in international maritime law that govern strait transit, shipping safety, and pollution prevention.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n1. THE MONTREUX CONVENTION (1936)\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• Key Focus: Regulates transit through the Turkish Straits (Bosporus and Dardanelles).\r\n• Key Rules:\r\n  - Guarantees complete freedom of passage for civilian vessels in peace and war.\r\n  - Limits the tonnage and duration of stay for non-Black Sea military warships (maximum 21 days, maximum 15,000 tons per vessel).\r\n  - Grants Turkey the authority to close the straits to all foreign warships during times of war or when threatened.\r\n• Debate Use: Crucial for debates on Black Sea security, grain corridors, and regional naval deployments.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n2. THE SUA CONVENTION (1988)\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• Title: Convention for the Suppression of Unlawful Acts against the Safety of Maritime Navigation.\r\n• Key Focus: Suppressing maritime terrorism, hijackings, and boarding of civilian ships.\r\n• Key Rules:\r\n  - Defines offenses including seizure of ships by force, acts of violence against persons on board, and placing destructive devices on ships.\r\n  - Requires state parties to prosecute or extradite suspects found in their territory.\r\n• Debate Use: Cite to argue for international legal accountability for Houthi attacks on merchant ships.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n3. THE SOLAS CONVENTION (1974)\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• Title: International Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea.\r\n• Key Focus: Minimum safety standards for merchant ship construction, equipment, and operation.\r\n• Key Rules:\r\n  - Requires commercial ships to carry active Automatic Identification System (AIS) transponders.\r\n  - Turning off AIS to hide shipping movements (common in shadow fleets) violates SOLAS safety rules.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n4. THE MARPOL CONVENTION (1973)\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• Title: International Convention for the Prevention of Pollution from Ships.\r\n• Key Focus: Preventing marine pollution from operational or accidental causes.\r\n• Key Rules:\r\n  - Regulates discharges of oil, chemicals, and sewage.\r\n  - Older, poorly maintained shadow fleet tankers operating without oil-spill liability insurance violate MARPOL guidelines.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\nOFFICIAL SOURCES\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• International Maritime Organization (IMO) Treaty Database\r\n• Montreux Convention Treaty texts (1936)\r\n================================================================\r\n"
