        "path":  "UNSC/Resources/UN Charter.txt",
        "name":  "UN Charter.txt",
        "size":  3841,
        "content":  "================================================================\r\nUNSC RESOURCE GUIDE: THE UN CHARTER \u0026 MARITIME CONFLICTS | BATTLE CARD\r\n================================================================\r\n\r\n⚡ QUICK REFERENCE\r\n   Committee: UNSC — Agenda: Geopolitical Conflicts, Supply Chains \u0026 Maritime Security\r\n   Key Articles: Art 2(4) — Force prohibition / Art 39 — Threat determination\r\n           Art 41 — Sanctions / Art 42 — Naval enforcement / Art 51 — Self-defense at sea\r\n           Art 51 is YOUR go-to for justifying naval escort operations\r\n\r\nThis document reviews how the Articles of the United Nations Charter apply to naval deployments, self-defense at sea, and trade sanctions.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n1. ARTICLE 2(4) - PROHIBITION OF THE USE OF FORCE\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• Text Summary: Member states must refrain from the threat or use of force against the territorial integrity or political independence of any state.\r\n• Maritime Application:\r\n  - Applicable to military blockades, firing upon civilian vessels, or unauthorized naval incursions in territorial waters.\r\n  - Reaffirms that international sea lanes must remain corridors of peace.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n2. ARTICLE 39 - DETERMINATION OF THREATS TO PEACE\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• Text Summary: The Security Council determines the existence of any threat to the peace, breach of the peace, or act of aggression and recommends or decides measures to restore order.\r\n• Maritime Application:\r\n  - The Council has declared systematic piracy (Somalia) and attacks on shipping (Red Sea) as breaches of international peace, triggering Security Council resolutions.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n3. ARTICLE 41 - NON-MILITARY SANCTIONS\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• Text Summary: Authorizes the Security Council to decide on measures not involving the use of armed force, including complete or partial interruption of economic relations and communications.\r\n• Maritime Application:\r\n  - Formulates the legal basis for UNSC-mandated trade embargoes, cargo restrictions, and financial transaction blocks.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n4. ARTICLE 42 - MILITARY ENFORCEMENT MEASURES\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• Text Summary: Should non-military measures prove inadequate, the Security Council may take such action by air, sea, or land forces as may be necessary to maintain or restore peace.\r\n• Maritime Application:\r\n  - Provides the legal basis for UN-authorized naval blockades or military interventions to enforce embargoes (e.g., historical Gulf War interceptions).\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n5. ARTICLE 51 - INHERENT RIGHT OF SELF-DEFENSE\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• Text Summary: Nothing in the Charter shall impair the inherent right of individual or collective self-defense if an armed attack occurs against a member state.\r\n• Maritime Application:\r\n  - Cited by states to justify firing upon hostile drones/missiles targeting commercial vessels (Operation Prosperity Guardian).\r\n  - Subject of intense debate over whether \"armed attacks\" can apply to non-military commercial shipping.\r\n\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\nOFFICIAL SOURCES\r\n----------------------------------------------------------------\r\n• Charter of the United Nations (1945)\r\n• Repertory of Practice of United Nations Organs (UNSC)\r\n================================================================\r\n"
